1180:\textbf{Remark on the classical bound.} Assuming $(M,\Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S$ outright recovers the classical statement $I(X;E)-I(S;E)\geq I(M,\Sigma;E)$ as a corollary of Part~(ii). Our form keeps the leakage $\varepsilon = I(M,\Sigma; S)$ explicit, and Part~(iv) measures it via both parametric (CCA) and non-parametric (KSG) methods, so the bound holds without any independence assumption. Appendix~\ref{app:mi_tightness} bypasses the bound entirely by directly measuring $H(E \mid S)$, confirming the gap $I(M,\Sigma; S \mid E)$ averages $0.075$ nats (Table~\ref{tab:mi_tightness}). The agreement between Gaussian reference and distribution-free KSG estimates ($\pm 0.26$ nats) confirms the CCA-based values are reliable despite non-Gaussianity.
