\section{Raw-Routed Mixture of Adapters (RR-MoA)}
\label{sec:method}

\textbf{Problem setup.} Given an input window $\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times c}$ ($T{=}512$ throughout, $c$ channels), forecasting predicts $\mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times c}$ at horizons $h\in\{96,192,336,720\}$ under MSE loss. We use a pretrained TSFM as a feature extractor $f_\theta:\mathbb{R}^{T\times c}\to\mathbb{R}^{P\times d}$ producing $P$ patch embeddings of dimension $d$, with parameters $\theta$ \emph{frozen} ($\nabla_\theta\equiv 0$), and train only an adapter $g_\phi:\mathbb{R}^{P\times d}\to\mathbb{R}^{h\times c}$ on per-task data, with $|\phi|\leq 500{,}000$ trainable parameters; the cap holds per-task storage under ${\sim}2$\,MB and enables the multi-tenant hot-swap regime in App.~\ref{app:deployment}. A \emph{per-window mixture of adapters} replaces the single $g_\phi$ with $K$ candidate experts $\{\mathrm{Expert}_j\}_{j=1}^K$ and a router $G_\psi$ that, on input $\mathbf{x}$, returns a sparse weight vector $\widetilde{w}\in\Delta^{K-1}$ supported on a Top-$k$ subset $\mathcal{T}\subset\{1,\ldots,K\}$ ($k\leq K$), yielding the prediction $\hat{\mathbf{y}}=\sum_{j\in\mathcal{T}}\widetilde{w}_j\,\mathrm{Expert}_j(f_\theta(\mathbf{x}))$. The training objective is $\min_{\phi,\psi}\,\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})}\,\|\hat{\mathbf{y}}-\mathbf{y}\|_2^2$. The design question this paper studies is what signal the router $G_\psi$ should read: prior work routes on the hidden states $\mathbf{H}\!=\!f_\theta(\mathbf{x})$, which we show fails on instance-normalized TSFMs (\S\ref{sec:main_results}). We also evaluate on $20\%$-masked imputation under the same formalism with $\mathbf{y}$ replaced by the masked reconstruction target.

\begin{figure}[t!]

