549	\vspace{-0.5em}
550	\section{Conclusion and Limitations}
551	\vspace{-0.4em}
552	
553	\textbf{Diagnosis and fix.} We identified \emph{normalization-induced routing collapse}: MoE adapters on instance-normalized TSFMs collapse to a single expert because the per-window normalization handling distribution shift also strips the $(\mu,\sigma)$ statistics the router needs. Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} formalizes the loss; $R(\mathcal{D})$ predicts it at $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ ($p{<}0.002$); eight causal controls (Table~\ref{tab:causal_controls}) rule out optimization, geometry, and routing-paradigm explanations. Three interventions confirm the diagnosis: RR-MoA wins $54/54$ cells ($26$--$79\%$); SR-MoA drops the external router and beats RR-MoA by $13$--$42\%$; Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+} closes the DLinear gap on $107/123$ cross-backbone cells. The mechanism replicates on ResNet/ViT MoE with InstanceNorm (Appendix~\ref{app:vision_moe}) and is absent from LayerNorm-only Timer-XL ($4$--$9\%$, $15$--$100{\times}$ smaller).
554	
555	\textbf{Limitations and outlook.} The diagnosis is specific to per-window normalizers that strip $(\mu,\sigma)$ (RevIN, BatchNorm1d, GroupNorm, InstanceNorm); LayerNorm/RMSNorm-only backbones need no fix. $R(\mathcal{D})$ is a monotone proxy, not a sharp threshold; intermediate-$R$ behavior at varying freeze levels is open. Evaluation covers forecasting and imputation; classification and anomaly detection remain. The fix needs raw input at routing time (adapter-style finetuning, not API-served models); the pool is fixed at five hand-coded experts (learned pools are open). $R(\mathcal{D})$ is portable: any MoE-adapter on a normalized backbone can compute it pre-training, surfacing an \emph{upstream architectural} cause downstream rescues have so far blocked.\label{page:end_main}
556	\bibliographystyle{plainnat}
557	\begin{thebibliography}{20}

